    "title": "Masters: Green Jacket club for champions only",
    "text": "April 7, 2009, 2:00 a.m. ETIt’s just a jacket, distinguished by its bright green hue and the patch on its left breast pocket but otherwise quite unremarkable as garments go.It does not represent the height of fashion.It does not go well with some colors and in fact can be quite unsightly when paired with the wrong shirt or trousers.Truth be told, if the jacket hung on the rack at a thrift shop — not that it ever would — it probably would get passed over by plenty of unsuspecting customers.But the Masters Tournament green jacket is, of course, more than a garment. In a world in which change is measured in nanoseconds, it represents tradition and sepia-toned history. In a sports landscape shaped by the almighty dollar, its value is intrinsic and incalculable.The jacket’s cachet, besides its connection to a more genteel era, is that it is worn by one of the most exclusive clubs in all of sports: Masters champions.In fact, going into the 73rd Masters Tournament this week, exactly as many men, 44, have won the green jacket as have taken the oath as president of the United States.As a symbol of singular achievement, the jacket has few equals in sports and none in golf, at least not in the United States. In some parts of the world the Claret Jug awarded the “champion golfer of the year” at the British Open is as highly coveted. But every golfer who aspires to greatness, from Asia to Australia to Argentina, dreams of wearing the Masters green jacket.“Everybody knows what it means,” said Jerry Kelly, who has played in six Masters and had his closest brush with immortality in 2007, when he tied for fifth. “There’s probably some players who are going to win the PGA Championship and not know the trophy is called the Wanamaker Trophy. There’s probably a lot of young players who don’t know what the Claret Jug is.“But all you’ve got to say is ’the jacket’ – you don’t even have to say ’green jacket’ – and people know what you’re talking about.”For a professional golfer, there is no bigger thrill than being helped into the champion’s green jacket late on a Sunday afternoon in April, as the sun sets behind the loblolly pines at the Augusta National Golf Club.It’s a scene that has been repeated every year for more than a half-century and has involved most of the game’s luminaries, from Hogan and Snead to Palmer and Nicklaus to Woods and Mickelson.The single-breasted jacket is made from 3 yards of tropical weight wool and polyester with a rayon lining. The Augusta National logo appears on a patch on the left breast pocket and also on three brass buttons. The color is that of a perfectly manicured fairway; it is most commonly called kelly green but officially is “Masters green.”If only it were so easy to describe the aura.“Well, it’s incredible that a garment – no disrespect by using that word – but it’s incredible that a piece of clothing has such tremendous history and mystique to it,” said Trevor Immelman, the defending Masters champion.“It always strikes me how people are so respectful of the green jacket and are in such awe of basically just being in its presence.”Late last year, Immelman was claiming his baggage at an airport in Japan when some Japanese golf fans recognized him. Then they spotted the green jacket he was carrying.“I mean, these guys started crying,” Immelman said. “They were so in awe of the fact that they were in the presence of the green jacket. To me, it was so humbling and so cool because these guys respected Augusta National and they respected the Masters and everything it stood for.“My caddie and I were like, ’Wow, this is out of the ordinary.’ But the more I thought about it, the more I realized that the mystique and the history that goes along with Augusta National is just something that not many sports have.”The first version of the green jacket, made by the Brooks Uniform Company of New York City, was worn by club members at the fourth Masters in 1937.Clifford Roberts, the taciturn and iron-fisted co-founder of Augusta National, urged members to buy the jackets (he usually referred to the garment as a “coat” in his writings) so that tournament attendees could easily find “a reliable source of information.”No one can recall who came up with the idea of bestowing a jacket and honorary club membership on the Masters champion, but Roberts and Bobby Jones would have signed off on it. In 1949, Sam Snead became the first recipient.Each year since then, the champion has been helped into his green jacket by the previous year’s winner at the Sunday afternoon awards ceremony.Members cannot remove their jackets from the Augusta National grounds and Masters champions can do so only for one year. Then the jacket must be returned to the club, where it is stored in a cedar closet.After Gary Player won the 1961 Masters, he took his green jacket home to South Africa, put it away and forgot to return it to the club. More than a year later, he got a call from Roberts.“He said, ’Gary, I believe you’ve taken the Masters jacket home,’ “ Player said. “I said, ’Yes, Mr. Roberts, I have it, but if you want it back you’ll have to come here and fetch it yourself.’ We had a good laugh about that. He said, ’Just do me a favor and don’t wear it out anywhere.’ “The Masters champion is allowed to wear his jacket to golf functions and appearances, but never in conjunction with any commercial advertising.Every year during Masters week the jackets are hung in the small and modestly appointed champions locker room. The room is off-limits to the other contestants, though many can’t resist opening the door for a quick peek.The past winners also wear their jackets to the annual champions’ dinner, a tradition started by Ben Hogan in 1952. The defending champion picks the menu, so it is Immelman’s turn this year.“I think that evening will probably end up being the highlight of the week for me,” he said. “To be able to rub shoulders with such great champions and be a part of such a great fraternity of champions is going to be an incredible moment for me.”Over the years, other tournaments have copied the Masters’ jacket tradition, though green is unofficially off-limits.Hogan was given a tartan jacket after he won consecutive events at Colonial in 1952 and ’53, and the tradition continues to this day at the Crowne Plaza Invitational. The Verizon Heritage also awards a tartan jacket, first given to the second winner, Bob Goalby, in 1970.The Arnold Palmer Invitational has awarded a jacket since the tournament debuted at Bay Hill in 1979; the color was changed from gray to blue in 1991.Other tournaments that award jackets are the Stanford St. Jude Championship (seersucker), the Memorial (gray), the FBR Open (blue) and the Quail Hollow Championship (light blue).There are probably hundreds of family golf outings and charity events across the country at which the winner solemnly dons an old, musty coat rescued from a second-hand clothing store.No one would ever confuse any of those jackets for the greatest prize in golf.“There are some other jackets out there,” Kelly said, “but there’s only one jacket.”",
